      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "In 38 patients with angina pectoris, 87 unipolar lead electrocardiograms were recorded from different sites over the entire thoracic surface both before and after submaximal treadmill exercise. The site of exercise-induced ST depression on the body surface was correlated with the findings of the coronary arteriography. The number of leads which showed exercise-induced ST depression was 7.2 +/- 1.8 in one vessel disease, 15.6 +/- 2.1 in two vessel disease, and 23.6 +/- 2.0 in three vessel disease (values are mean +/- SEM). Thus, the number of leads showing ST depression was proportional to the severity of the coronary artery disease. Exercise-induced ST depression was most often seen in left anterior chest leads, especially in V5 of the standard 12-ECG. However, it was not possible to identify the obstructed coronary artery from the body surface distribution of ST depression."},
